@section Recursion and the call stack

This section covers recursive and iterative processes in this language, and
how tail calls or loops express them. It is the grounding for @ref{1.2},
where the book first separates a recursive process from an iterative one.

@heading Recursion takes a stack

A plain recursive call takes a stack frame and holds it until the calls it
spawned return. The tree-recursive @code{fib} of @ref{1.2.2} fans out
exactly this way:

@example typescript
const factorial = (n: number): number =>
  n <= 1 ? 1 : n * factorial(n - 1);

const fib = (n: number): number => (n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2));

factorial(18)
// => 6402373705728000

fib(10)
// => 55
@end example

@noindent
@code{factorial(18)} is the last factorial the @code{number} type holds
exactly (@ref{0.2}); @code{19!} crosses the @code{2 ** 53} bound and moves
to @code{bigint}. TypeScript gives the book's processes no tail-call
guarantee: the language standard reserves none, and no engine this book
runs on performs one in practice. A linear-recursive @code{factorial}
written as above is a linear-recursive process here too, and the engine's
stack bounds how deep it may run; past the bound it raises a
@code{RangeError} instead of returning a wrong answer. Exercise 0.3
measures the bound.

@heading Loops make the iteration

The iterative process of @ref{1.2.1} is expressed with a loop: the state
the recursive version kept in pending multiplications lives in local
variables instead, and the stack stops growing:

@example typescript
const factorialIter = (n: number): number => @{
  let acc = 1;
  for (let k = 2; k <= n; k += 1) @{
    acc *= k;
  @}
  return acc;
@};

factorialIter(18)
// => 6402373705728000
@end example

@example typescript
const sumTo = (n: number): number => @{
  let total = 0;
  let k = 1;
  while (k <= n) @{
    total += k;
    k += 1;
  @}
  return total;
@};

sumTo(100)
// => 5050
@end example

@noindent
One consequence shapes the rest of the book. A loop rewrites only the
shape the programmer writes, so a tree-recursive process such as
@code{fib} stays tree-recursive, and deep non-tail recursion such as the
tree walks of @ref{1.2} and @ref{2.2.2} stays bounded by the stack. The
evaluators of @ref{4.1} say plainly that they are not tail recursive, as
the book's own dialect does, and @ref{5.4} restores tail recursion in the
explicit-control evaluator.

@heading Deferring work: generators

A @newterm{generator} records how to compute a sequence and runs that
work one step at a time, at each pull. @code{function*} declares one, and
@code{yield} hands out the next element and pauses:

@example typescript
function* naturals(): Generator<number> @{
  let n = 1;
  for (;;) @{
    yield n;
    n += 1;
  @}
@}

function* squaresOf(g: Generator<number>): Generator<number> @{
  for (const x of g) @{
    yield x * x;
  @}
@}

function* take<A>(g: Generator<A>, n: number): Generator<A> @{
  let k = 0;
  for (const x of g) @{
    if (k >= n) @{
      return;
    @}
    yield x;
    k += 1;
  @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
Nothing is computed until something pulls:

@example typescript
Array.from(take(squaresOf(naturals()), 5))
// => [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
@end example

@noindent
The intermediate steps record work, and each pull drives exactly one
element out of the pipeline. Cold means recomputing: a generator holds a
recipe, not a row of results, so walking a fresh one runs the recipe
again from the start.

The streams of @ref{3.5} need two properties a generator does not have.
They are memoized, so two cursors over one stream force each element
once; and they permit self-reference, where a tail must capture the very
stream being defined. Chapter 3 therefore ships a second type, the
memoized @code{LazyList} of @ref{3.5.1}, and the rule this book follows
from @ref{3.5} onward: a generator carries a pipeline whose elements are
consumed as they are produced, and a stream carries a shared, memoized
structure that may observe and reuse its own past.

