@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.3}Exercise 0.3:} This section said a loop
carries its state in locals while a plain recursion takes a frame per
call. Implement @code{sumToRecursive(n)} as a recursion that takes a
frame per call and @code{sumToIterative(n)} as a loop over the same sum;
the two agree on inputs the recursion survives. Then write
@code{recursionDepth()}, a probe that descends until the engine refuses
and returns the last completed depth, catching the @code{RangeError} and
rethrowing anything else. Test that @code{sumToIterative(100000)} is
@code{5000050000} while @code{sumToRecursive(100000)} raises a
@code{RangeError}, and that @code{recursionDepth()} reports a finite
depth above 1000 on this machine.

